<p class="lead">
  Wide margins around scanned pages, exported slides with excess white space, or a PDF designed for a paper size larger than needed — all waste screen space and printer ink for content that isn't actually there. Removing the margins fixes it without touching the actual content.
</p>

<h2>Crop margins in your browser (no upload, free)</h2>
<p>
  The <a href="/tools/crop-pdf/">Crop PDF tool</a> on keptlocal removes margins from every page of a document, entirely on your device.
</p>
<ol>
  <li>Drop your PDF onto the tool.</li>
  <li>Set the margin amount to remove — uniformly or per side.</li>
  <li>Download the cropped result.</li>
</ol>
<p>
  The crop applies to the entire document in one pass, so a multi-page PDF with consistent margins throughout gets cleaned up without needing to process each page individually.
</p>

<h2>Common reasons a PDF has excess margins</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Scans</strong> — a physical document scanned on a flatbed scanner often includes extra white space around the actual page content, from imprecise placement on the scanner bed.</li>
  <li><strong>Slide or presentation exports</strong> — exporting slides to PDF sometimes adds letterboxing or margin space to fit a fixed page size that doesn't match the slide's actual aspect ratio.</li>
  <li><strong>Print-oriented page sizes</strong> — a document formatted for a specific paper size (Letter or A4) but with content that only fills a portion of it, common in documents originally designed for print with generous margins that don't serve any purpose on screen.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What actually changes when you crop</h2>
<p>
  Cropping adjusts a PDF page's crop box — the boundary that determines what's actually visible and printed — without touching the underlying content stream. Anything within the new, smaller boundary displays and prints exactly as before, fully selectable and searchable if it was before; anything outside the new boundary is no longer shown. This is different from re-rendering or resizing the page's content, which is why cropping is fast and doesn't introduce any quality loss — no pixels are recompressed, no text is reflowed, only the visible window onto the page changes.
</p>

<h2>Privacy: what happens to your file</h2>
<p>
  Cropping runs entirely in your browser using pdf-lib — your document is never uploaded.
</p>

